  {"box":[-10,-9.5,2.4,0.4,0.4,4.2],"m":"corten"}],"notes":"From the four elevations (a low flat-roofed box of board-formed concrete, about 16 x 14 m and one tall storey, with a slab-like concrete chimney rising off-centre), the downhill views (the house standing on a plinth of rammed stone that holds a lower unit, with a concrete stair up its side and a round stone-rimmed pool below), the uphill view (single storey on a raised stone terrace with a planter and sliding expanded-metal shutters), the interiors (glass walls at the back of deep carved galleries, and the triangular skylight in the middle of the roof) and the article (three galleries toward three views, 40 cm stone plinth absorbing the slope). The downhill, pool side faces south. The slope is shown as a shelf of gravel ground at the plinth's height on the uphill (north) side. The plan's exact angles are guessed orthogonal (the article calls the volume polyhedral; the photographs show near-square corners), as are the galleries' widths and depths, the lower unit's openings and the scrub's positions. Expanded-metal shutters are drawn 'dark', the nearest to the mesh as it reads."}